theoryMore:
**Points, pixels and @2x/@3x.** On iOS you design in points (a logical unit), and each point becomes several pixels depending on the screen: on a 2x screen, 1 point = 2x2 pixels; on a 3x screen, 3x3 pixels. An image of 100x100 points therefore needs 100x100 px (@1x), 200x200 px (@2x) and 300x300 px (@3x); without the extra files it would look blurry. The '@2x' and '@3x' suffixes in the file name mark these versions, and inside the image set you just drag each file into the right slot. A vector image (PDF or SVG, with 'Preserve Vector Data') or an SF Symbol scales to any size without losing sharpness, so a single version is enough.

**Dark mode.** In the Attributes inspector of the image set (or color set), set Appearances = Any, Dark. A second slot appears: put the dark version there. The system picks automatically according to the device mode, with no 'if' in the code.

**Assets vs SF Symbols vs loose files.** Each thing has its own call: `Image("logo")` looks up an asset-catalog item with that name; `Image(systemName: "heart.fill")` asks for a system SF Symbol (in UIKit, `UIImage(systemName:)`); `Image("heart.fill")` will NOT find the symbol, because it would look for an asset. A file that is not an image, such as a JSON, is read from the bundle (the folder packaged with the app): 

`Bundle.main.url(forResource:withExtension:)` returns a `URL?`, because the file may not be there:
if let url = Bundle.main.url(forResource: "config", withExtension: "json"),
   let data = try? Data(contentsOf: url) {
    print(data.count)
}

**A wrong name is not a compile error.** The name in `Image("Logotipo")` is just a string resolved at run time: if no asset has that name, the image simply does not appear (and `UIImage(named:)` returns nil). The generated symbols from Xcode 15, such as `Image(.logo)`, turn this silent failure into a compile error. Likewise, if you forget the target membership, the app compiles fine, but `Bundle.main.url` returns nil on the device because the file was not copied into the bundle.

**Launch screen and AppIcon.** The app icon is the AppIcon item in the catalog. The launch screen is a static screen shown briefly while the app loads; it does not ask for permissions, store data or show ads.

**Reference cycle and `[weak self]`.** ARC frees an object when no strong reference points to it. If a screen stores a closure in a property and that closure captures `self` strongly, each holds the other: neither is freed (a memory leak). `[weak self]` captures `self` weakly: it becomes an Optional that is nil once the screen goes away, which is why you write `self?.`:
final class Tela {
    var titulo = "perfil"
    var acao: (() -> Void)?
    func configurar() {
        acao = { [weak self] in print(self?.titulo ?? "nil") }
    }
}

**`@escaping`.** A closure passed as a parameter is, by default, non-escaping: it must finish before the function returns. If the function stores it (for example `tarefas.append(f)`) or runs it later (a network response), the parameter must be marked `@escaping`, otherwise the compiler complains.

**Default values and functions as values.** Parameters with a default value may be omitted at the call, but the remaining ones keep their order and labels: in `f(b: 5)`, `a` uses the default. A function assigned to a variable becomes a value of type `(Int, Int) -> Void`, and calling through that variable uses no labels:
func mover(de origem: Int, para destino: Int) { print(origem, destino) }
let g = mover       // tipo (Int, Int) -> Void
g(1, 2)             // no labels
func f(a: Int = 1, b: Int = 2) -> Int { a * 10 + b }
print(f(b: 5))      // 15: a uses the default 1

**Captured variables and named tuples.** A closure keeps the variables of the scope where it was born and keeps them alive between calls; each call to `criar()` returns a closure with its own `n`:
func criar() -> () -> Int {
    var n = 0
    return { n += 1; return n }
}
let c1 = criar(), c2 = criar()
_ = c1(); print(c1(), c2())   // 2 1: each closure has its own n
A function that returns `(min: Int, max: Int)` lets you read the result by name: `r.max`.

**Parameters are constants.** Inside a function, every parameter is a constant, even a struct with `var` properties: changing `p.x` is an error. To change the caller's variable, the parameter must be `inout` and the call takes `&` (more in 'Functions and Closures in Depth'):
struct Ponto { var x = 0 }
func f(_ p: Ponto) { p.x = 1 }              // error: 'p' is a constant
func g(_ p: inout Ponto) { p.x = 1 }        // OK
var q = Ponto(); g(&q); print(q.x)          // 1

**The memberwise initialiser in detail.** Properties with a default value become optional parameters, but the argument order follows the declaration and labels are mandatory; properties without a default are required:
struct Pessoa { var nome: String; var idade = 0 }
Pessoa(nome: "Ana")                  // OK: idade uses its default
Pessoa(nome: "Ana", idade: 30)       // OK
// Pessoa(idade: 30, nome: "Ana")    // error: order differs from the declaration
// Pessoa()                          // error: nome has no default

**Read-only computed property and nested type.** A computed property with only `get` (or just the body) is read-only: assigning to it is an error. To accept assignment you must write a `set`. A type declared inside another is accessed by its full name from outside (`Pedido.Status`); inside `Pedido`, `Status` is enough:
struct Circulo {
    var raio = 1.0
    var diametro: Double { raio * 2 }        // get only: read-only
}
var c = Circulo()
// c.diametro = 10                            // compile error
struct Pedido { enum Status { case novo, pago } }
let s: Pedido.Status = .pago                  // from outside: Pedido.Status

**Copying arrays of structs.** An array is also a value: `var copia = lista; copia[0].x = 5` changes only the copy; `lista[0].x` keeps the old value.

**When to prefer class.** Besides shared identity and inheritance, Apple cites Objective-C interoperability: classes that inherit from `NSObject`, required by certain older APIs, can only be classes. For small data without identity, such as a coordinate (latitude and longitude), struct is the right choice.

**`let` with class.** A class constant fixes the reference, not the contents: you can change the object's `var` properties, but you cannot point the constant at another object. Since `a` and `b` point to the same instance, changing through one shows up in the other. (With a struct, by contrast, `let` freezes the whole instance.)
final class Caixa { var v = 0 }
let a = Caixa()
let b = a
b.v = 5                 // OK: changes the shared object
print(a.v, a === b)     // 5 true
// a = Caixa()          // error: the reference is constant

**Raw value vs associated value in enums.** A raw value is a fixed value per case, of the same type for all, defined at the declaration; in an `Int` enum without explicit values it counts from 0, and after an explicit value it continues from there. In a `String` enum, the default raw value is the case name itself. `init?(rawValue:)` returns an Optional. An associated value, by contrast, stores different data each time the case is created:
enum Planeta: Int { case mercurio = 1, venus, terra }
print(Planeta.terra.rawValue)        // 3 (counts up from the last given value)
print(Planeta(rawValue: 2) as Any)   // Optional(venus)
enum Estado {
    case carregando
    case sucesso([String])           // associated value: different data per use
    case erro(String)
}
That is why an enum such as `Estado` (loading, success, error) is the natural choice for a screen's states.

**`private` vs `fileprivate`.** `private` limits access to the body of the declaration and its extensions in the same file; `fileprivate` opens up the whole file, including other types in it:
class A {
    private var s = 1
}
extension A {
    func ler() -> Int { s }    // OK: an extension in the same file sees the private member
}

**`==` and `===` on classes.** `===` only asks whether two references are the same object. `==` requires the type to adopt `Equatable` (a class does not get `==` for free) and compares values. Finally, for other modules to inherit and override you need `open`; `public` only allows use.

**Key paths: types and use.** `\Pessoa.nome` reads nothing: it is a value that describes the path to the property. If `nome` is `let`, the type is `KeyPath<Pessoa, String>` (read-only); if it is `var`, it is `WritableKeyPath` (read and write). You use it with `[keyPath:]`. Optional chaining `apelido?.count` stops at the first nil and returns an Optional (`Int?`):
struct Pes { let nome: String; var idade: Int; var apelido: String? }
let k1 = \Pes.nome     // KeyPath<Pes, String>          (let: read-only)
let k2 = \Pes.idade    // WritableKeyPath<Pes, Int>     (var: read and write)
let p = Pes(nome: "Ana", idade: 30, apelido: nil)
print(p[keyPath: k1])        // Ana
print(p.apelido?.count as Any)   // nil: the chain stops at the first nil

**A `didSet` that assigns to its own property.** Assigning to a property inside its own observer does not call the observer again, so there is no recursion; it is a common way to cap a value:
struct Contador {
    var n = 0 {
        didSet { if n > 5 { n = 5 } }   // assigning here does not call didSet again
    }
}
var c = Contador(); c.n = 9; print(c.n)   // 5

**A complete property wrapper.** The wrapper needs `wrappedValue` (read and write of the real value). An `init(wrappedValue:)` receives the initial value written in the declaration: in `@Limitado var vidas = 3`, the 3 arrives at that init. Later assignments go through the setter, where the limit is applied:
@propertyWrapper
struct Limitado {
    private var v: Int
    var wrappedValue: Int {
        get { v }
        set { v = min(newValue, 10) }      // setter: caps at 10
    }
    init(wrappedValue: Int) { v = min(wrappedValue, 10) }   // initial value
}
struct Jogo { @Limitado var vidas = 3 }    // chama init(wrappedValue: 3)
var j = Jogo(); j.vidas = 15; print(j.vidas)   // 10

**`projectedValue` and SwiftUI's wrappers.** A wrapper can offer a second value, the `projectedValue`, accessed with `$`. In `@State var contador = 0`, `contador` is the value (Int) and `$contador` is a `Binding<Int>`, a read-write connection to that state. `@Binding` is the wrapper a child view uses to receive that connection from its parent (it receives `$contador`); `@Environment` reads shared values from the view's environment; `@AppStorage` stores in UserDefaults.

**`lazy`.** In `lazy var dados = carregarDados()`, the value is computed only at first access, and this requires `var`, because the property changes from 'no value yet' to 'has a value'.
